In some article stuckey@mrcnext.cso.uiuc.edu (Anthony J. Stuckey) writes:
->Now on to the comments.
->	This entire line of sight algorithm thing should
->be taken out back and shot.

Why so?  I rather like it.  It makes more sense to me than the algorithm
that I've seen used in Moria, where you can't see anything in a room until you
walk into it, at which point everything is visible if you apply light to it...

->	Same for the idea of corpses remaining on the map until
->you walk past them again.

Hm.  I like this too.  The idea is that you remember there having been a
corpse there, but you can't know whether it's rotted away or not until you
go back there.  It also happens for items that you've seen but that some
monster picks up and moves.  You thought there was something there, but it
disappears by the time you've gotten there to look for it.

